1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A file is a named sequence of bytes. The name, the metadata and the bytes live in three different places.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Three tables: the directory maps a name to an inode number, the inode maps an inode number to blocks.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Opening one file costs three block reads here: the directory block, the inode, and the first data block.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Every later question is one of: where do the blocks go, how big is the table, how many accesses does it cost.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Contiguous allocation gives the file one run of blocks. Fast to read, and it fragments the disk.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Linked allocation stores the next block number inside each block. No external fragmentation, and no random access.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Indexed allocation puts all the pointers in one index block, so block k is one hop from the inode.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
GATE 2017 Set 2: with no external fragmentation allowed, linked and indexed qualify, contiguous does not.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
A file allocation table lifts the chain out of the blocks: entry i holds the block after block i.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
GATE 2014 Set 2: 100,000 blocks of 1,000 bytes, so the table is 400,000 bytes and the data is 99.6 MB.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
GATE 2025 Set 1: a 4-byte pointer in every 64 KB block leaves 65,532 usable bytes, and 65,468 wasted.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The table is the price of the scheme. Count its blocks before you count the data.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
An inode holds a fixed number of pointers: some direct, then single, double and triple indirect.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
GATE 2019: 4 KB blocks and 4-byte addresses give 1,024 pointers per block, so the largest file is 4.0 GB.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
GATE 2012: 128-byte blocks with 8-byte addresses give 16 pointers, and the answer is 35 KB, not 280.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The deepest term dominates. GATE 2004: with a triple indirect, 2^34 bytes, and the other terms vanish.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Insert a block after the fiftieth of a hundred: contiguous must shift the rest, fifty reads and fifty-one writes.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Linked allocation walks to the fiftieth block, writes the new block, and patches one pointer: 52 accesses.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
GATE 2022: 101 plus 52 is 153 disk accesses for the same insertion in the two schemes.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Indexed allocation pays two accesses, or one if the index block is already in memory. State the assumption.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A bitmap spends one bit per block. A 1 TB disk of 4 KB blocks has 2^28 blocks, so the bitmap is 32 MB.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The bitmap is easy to read and cheap to search for runs of free blocks; the list is smaller only when the disk is full.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
GATE 2026 Set 2: a list block holds (2,048 - 4) / 4 = 511 numbers, so 8,388,608 free blocks need 16,417 list blocks.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Grouping stores many free numbers per block and keeps one pointer; counting stores one count per run of free blocks.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Each component of a path is a directory lookup. Three components and no cache cost six block reads.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
A linear-list directory is searched entry by entry, so its cost is the position of the name, not a constant.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
GATE 2021 Set 1: creating and renaming need a full scan, because both must prove the name is free.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Mounting attaches a file system at a directory, so one tree can span several volumes.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A hard link is a second name for the same inode, so the link count becomes two and no data is copied.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Deleting one name drops the count to one; the data is freed only when the count reaches zero.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
A symbolic link is a new inode holding a path, so it can dangle and it can cross file systems.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Hard links cannot cross a file system, because the inode number is only unique within one volume.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A 10,000-byte file in 4 KB blocks occupies three blocks, and 2,288 bytes of the last one are wasted.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The inode table is a fixed array, so a volume can run out of files while it still has free blocks.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Larger blocks raise throughput and waste more of the last block; the choice is a trade, not an optimum.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Space questions are always about three things: the last block, the pointer, and the table.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: convert everything to blocks first, rounding up, and write the block count down.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: pointers per block is block size divided by address size, then sum the direct and indirect terms.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: for free space, pick bits or entries, subtract the link, and take a ceiling.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Five traps: block count answered as bytes, the pointer forgotten, 512 instead of 511, the full-scan word, and the link count.

